    "evidence_text": "Our word recognition models build upon the RNN-based semi-character word recognition model due to  .\n\nWhile our word recognizers are trained on domain-specific text from the task at hand, they often predict UNK at test time, owing to the small domain-specific vocabulary.\n\nSimilarly,  studied how synthetic and natural noise affects character-level machine translation.\n\nScRNN with Backoff\tWe now describe semi-character RNNs for word recognition, explain their limitations, and suggest techniques to improve them.\n\nInspired by the psycholinguistic studies  ,  ,  proposed a semi-character based RNN (ScRNN) that processes a sentence of words with misspelled characters, predicting the correct words at each step.\n\nAt each sequence step, the training target is the correct corresponding word (output dimension equal to vocabulary size), and the model is optimized with cross-entropy loss.\n\nBelow, we explore different ways to back off when the ScRNN predicts UNK (a frequent outcome for rare and unseen words): Pass-through:\n\nWe refer the reader to  for comparisons of ScRNN to other anonymized commercial spell checkers.\n\nFor the ScRNN model, we use a single-layer Bi-LSTM with a hidden dimension size of 50.",
